<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congress of Erfurt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> was a meeting between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a> held in Erfurt from 27 September to 14 October 1808, attended by many German princes. It was meant to renew the alliance agreed at Tilsit, but Alexander would not commit himself against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, encouraged in secret by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a>. During the congress Napoleon received Goethe in a famous audience.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congress-of-erfurt.jpg" alt="Congress of Erfurt" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Gosse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entrevue_Erfurt_by_Nicolas_Grosse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The meeting was meant to reaffirm the alliance concluded the previous year at Tilsit after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fourth-coalition">War of the Fourth Coalition</a>. Napoleon had made an admirer of Alexander there, but by 1808 anti-French feeling at the Russian court was beginning to threaten the alliance. Napoleon and his foreign minister, Jean-Baptiste Nompère de Champagny, sought to strengthen it in order to settle affairs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and prepare for the expected war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Charles Maurice de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand-Périgord</a>, convinced that Napoleon was leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to destruction, worked against them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> was then under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s direct control as the Principality of Erfurt, and he set out to impress Alexander with the glories of his empire. The meeting grew into a gathering of kings, princes, dukes, barons and notables from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The actor Talma and the whole Comédie-Française presented sixteen French tragedies, and the twenty-year-old Arthur Schopenhauer arrived in Goethe's train and watched the proceedings with a cynical eye.</p><p>The outcome was the Erfurt Convention of fourteen articles. It called on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to end its war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, recognised the Russian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and stated that in case of war with Austria Russia should aid France &quot;to the best of its ability&quot;. The two emperors left for home on 14 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> began six months later, and Alexander gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as little help as he could. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> was the last meeting of the two emperors, and by 1810 each was considering war with the other. By 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> no longer complied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a>, had raised its defence spending and had moved troops to the border to prepare an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> struck first and with greater force.</p><p>From Tilsit onwards few observers believed the two powers could live side by side with the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, a French satellite, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> may have delayed the war, but conflict between them was likely in the end. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a> refers to the congress in War and Peace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congress of Erfurt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From September to October 1808 Napoleon held the <b>Congress of Erfurt</b> there, meeting Tsar Alexander I to renew their alliance and receiving Goethe in a famous audience.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-erfurt">Principality of Erfurt</a></li></ul></section>
